From 57b192c70ead59f2a8aa8b3134e70caa4eaec1ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 16:56:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] [Core] Release weights for a process checkpoint with
 --checkpoint-release-weights

With --checkpoint-release-weights, /checkpoint/prepare also discards the
weights after the KV cache, so a process checkpoint does not contain them,
and /checkpoint/restore wakes the weights and refills them through the
model loader before the KV cache. A checkpoint of a large model then stays
small, and a loader such as ModelExpress can refill the weights from a
running peer through reload_weights_inplace.

Worker.discard(("weights",)) does for the weights what a level-2 sleep
does: parameters a reload rewrites are dropped, and buffers and the
parameters --sleep-preserve-parameter-names keeps are backed up to host
memory, which wake_up(["weights"]) restores. The sleep backend and the
allocators take a tags filter for that, so the runtime and CUDA graph
allocations a checkpoint still needs stay mapped.

Signed-off-by: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Assisted-by: Claude
